digraph service_flow {
  rankdir=TB;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];

  caller [label="server.js API handlers\\n(6 routes)", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];

  subgraph cluster_gates {
    label="Shared validation gates (every call)";
    style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF"; fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=11;

    envcheck [label="resolveConfiguredRoot()\\nFILES_ROOT missing → 503\\nFILES_ROOT_MISSING", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
    rootstat [label="getRoot()\\nstat + isDirectory\\n503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND /\\nFILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
    struct   [label="assertCategoryStructure()\\n503 FILES_ROOT_EMPTY /\\nFILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
    pathwall [label="resolveInsideRoot()\\n. · .. · NUL → 400 INVALID_PATH\\noutside root → 403 PATH_TRAVERSAL", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_ops {
    label="Domain operations";
    style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF"; fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=11;

    cats    [label="listCategories()\\n+ summarizeTree() per category", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
    listing [label="listPath()\\nreadVisibleEntries + fs.stat per entry", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
    dl      [label="getDownloadTarget()\\nstat → {absPath,fileName,size,mtime}", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
    search  [label="searchEntries()\\niterative DFS · nameMatches\\nSEARCH_LIMIT · MAX_WALK_ENTRIES", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
    rel     [label="listLatestReleases()\\nrecursive walk · sort by mtime", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
    peek    [label="peekRootSync()\\nfsSync · never throws", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
  }

  disk [label="Real filesystem\\n(FILES_ROOT tree)", shape=cylinder, fillcolor="#FFEDD5"];
  out  [label="Plain data structures /\\nserviceError-stamped rejections", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
  back [label="server.js\\nsendJSON · streamDownload · sendServiceError", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];

  caller -> cats;
  caller -> listing;
  caller -> dl;
  caller -> search;
  caller -> rel;
  caller -> peek [style=dashed, label="sync, no gates"];

  cats -> envcheck;
  listing -> envcheck;
  dl -> envcheck;
  search -> envcheck;
  rel -> envcheck;
  envcheck -> rootstat;
  cats -> struct;
  search -> struct;
  rel -> struct;
  listing -> pathwall;
  dl -> pathwall;
  rootstat -> disk [style=dashed, label="validated root"];
  struct -> disk;
  pathwall -> disk;
  search -> disk;
  rel -> disk;
  cats -> out;
  listing -> out;
  dl -> out;
  search -> out;
  rel -> out;
  peek -> out;
  out -> back;
}

sect2.para:
🎯 Purpose
Validate that the configured root actually exists and is a directory, returning the validated absolute root used by every async operation.

🧒 ELI5
Before handing over any list, the keeper walks to the labelled door and actually opens it: locked/missing → "the room isn't there" (503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND); behind it is a closet, not a room → "that's not
a room" (503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY). Only then does work begin.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


Call resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot) (fail-fast on missing env).

await fs.stat(root) on the promise API. Any rejection — ENOENT, permission errors, a file where the path should be, even a dangling junction — is collapsed into one user-facing error 503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND (the raw catch {} deliberately discards the original cause; the operator is pointed at the .env path, which is the fix in essentially all cases).

Check stat.isDirectory(); a regular file or symlink-to-file at FILES_ROOT throws 503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY.

Return the validated root string. Note there is no memoisation: every API call re-stats, so an operator can fix FILES_ROOT without restarting the server, at the cost of one stat per request.



📥 Inputs


projectRoot (from server.js), process.env.FILES_ROOT.

Disk: the configured root directory (existence + type only).



📤 Outputs


Promise of the validated absolute root path.

Throws 503 FILES_ROOT_MISSING / FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND / FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY.



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: resolveConfiguredRoot, fs/promises, serviceError.

Consumed by: the first gate of listCategories, listPath, getDownloadTarget, searchEntries, listLatestReleases.




server.js — the /api/health route and the startup banner use the sync twin peekRootSync instead, because those call sites cannot await, and the sync variant intentionally never throws.

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
The distinction between the three 503 codes is operator-facing gold: collapse them into one "bad root" error and misconfigured deployments become guesswork. Adding a memoised root cache would remove the "fix `.env` and just retry" behaviour. Swapping fs.stat for fsSync.statSync here would make a missing root crash the request path synchronously inside async handlers — the async/throw boundary must stay intact for server.js's catch to convert these into JSON errors rather than unhandled rejections.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctiongetRoot(projectRoot){
constroot=resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot);
letstat;
try{
stat=awaitfs.stat(root);
}catch{
throwserviceError(
503,
"FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND",
"FILES_ROOTdoesnotexist.Checkthepathinyour.envfile."
);
}
if(!stat.isDirectory()){
throwserviceError(
503,
"FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY",
"FILES_ROOTexistsbutisnotadirectory."
);
}
returnroot;
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
The path-safety wall: convert a validated segment list into an absolute target path that is provably inside FILES_ROOT, or refuse loudly.

🧒 ELI5
The door guard who checks the request slip twice. First he reads each room name for forbidden scribbles (. — this room, .. — the room behind us, invisible zero-characters). Then, after walking the route, he checks the map again: "am I still inside the storage room?" If the visitor cleverly wrote a path that ends up outside, he tears up the slip: 403 PATH_TRAVERSAL.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


splitRel(relPath) normalises the input (above).

Reject explicitly: any segment equal to . or .., or containing a NUL byte (\0) → 400 INVALID_PATH. This is the readable, intention-revealing rejection — a client probing with .. gets a precise 400 rather than a vague 403.

path.resolve(root, ...parts) computes the absolute target. Because hostile segments were removed, this is expected to stay inside — but it is not trusted.

Defence in depth: path.relative(root, target) computes the walk from the root back to the target. If the result starts with .. (the target is outside or a sibling of the root) or is absolute (different drive letter on Windows), the target escaped → throw 403 PATH_TRAVERSAL. This catches anything step 2 could not anticipate — e.g. future Windows device-path quirks, or a root itself containing symlinked directories (note: path.resolve is purely lexical; symlink content is not followed here, only real fs.stat results by callers).

Return { target, parts, relPosix } — the absolute path for disk calls, the clean segments (so callers know depth and final name), and a canonical POSIX relative path for building API-facing path values.



📥 Inputs


root: validated absolute root (from getRoot).

relPath: raw client path from the URL or ?path= query.



📤 Outputs


{ target, parts, relPosix } on success.

Throws 400 INVALID_PATH or 403 PATH_TRAVERSAL.



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: splitRel, path, serviceError.

Consumed by: listPath and getDownloadTarget — every route where a client picks the path. Category-only endpoints (listCategories, searchEntries, listLatestReleases) never accept a client path and therefore never pass through here.

Sideways: server.js contains a second, independent traversal guard (isInsideRoot, used by serveStatic) for the static-asset tree — two walls for two trees, neither relying on the other.




server.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
This is the module's most security-critical block. Weakening any step — e.g. allowing .. because "the frontend never sends it", or skipping the path.relative re-check — converts /api/download into an arbitrary file read of the server (C:\..., /etc/passwd, the .env itself). Replacing path.relative with target.startsWith(root) reintroduces a classic bypass (the sibling directory /data/downloads-evil starts with /data/downloads). Even renaming the PATH_TRAVERSAL code is a public API change. Treat every edit here as a security review, not a refactor.

💻 Live Source Code:
functionresolveInsideRoot(root,relPath){
constparts=splitRel(relPath);
if(parts.some((p)=>p==="."||p===".."||p.includes("\0"))){
throwserviceError(400,"INVALID_PATH","Invalidpath.");
}
consttarget=path.resolve(root,...parts);
constrel=path.relative(root,target);
if(rel.startsWith("..")||path.isAbsolute(rel)){
throwserviceError(403,"PATH_TRAVERSAL","RequestedpathisoutsideFILES_ROOT.");
}
return{
target,
parts,
relPosix:parts.join("/"),
};
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
Compute a category card's statistics — total visible files, total subfolders, and the most recent modification anywhere beneath the category — the numbers displayed on the home page cards.

🧒 ELI5
Counting one shelf properly: walk every aisle under it, tally boxes and items, and jot down the newest "last touched" date you see. You skip drawers that won't open (unreadable folders) and never go deeper than twelve floors — you'd rather report an approximate count than never come back.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


Zero-initialise fileCount, folderCount, latestModified.

Define the recursive walk(current, depth); the very first guard is if (depth > MAX_WALK_DEPTH) return; — deep trees are silently truncated (documented behaviour, not an error).

fs.readdir(current, { withFileTypes: true }) inside try/catch: an unreadable subdirectory simply ends that branch (skip, don't fail the whole summary).

Per entry: isHidden skips it; directories increment folderCount and recurse; files increment fileCount and get an extra fs.stat(full) to read mtimeMs, keeping the running maximum. (Directories contribute only their children's mtimes — a folder's own rename does not count as content freshness.)

The per-file stat failure is swallowed (/* skip unreadable *\/) — a file vanishing mid-walk must not lose the whole category card.

Return { fileCount, folderCount, latestModified }, with latestModified normalised to null when nothing was stat-able (so callers can fall back).



📥 Inputs


dir: absolute category directory path.

Disk: the entire subtree, depth-capped.



📤 Outputs


Promise of { fileCount, folderCount, latestModified } (latestModified = epoch ms or null).



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: fs/promises, path, isHidden, MAX_WALK_DEPTH.

Consumed by: listCategories (one call per category, run concurrently via the surrounding Promise.all).

Sideways: the output feeds mapCategory in resource-service.js, whose stats.total (files + folders) and stats.latestModified (epoch ms) come straight from here and are rendered by home.js cards.




assets/js/services/resource-service.js




assets/js/home.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
The returned shape is a cross-layer contract (three field names are mapped by the frontend). Raising MAX_WALK_DEPTH here without touching the search/releases walkers makes the home-page counts disagree with search results on deep trees — an inconsistency users will report. Making this stricter (throwing on unreadable branches) turns one bad subfolder into a 503 for the whole home page. Its cost profile also matters: one stat per file, no cache — a category with 50,000 files means 50,000 stats per /api/categories call.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctionsummarizeTree(dir){
letfileCount=0;
letfolderCount=0;
letlatestModified=0;

asyncfunctionwalk(current,depth){
if(depth>MAX_WALK_DEPTH)return;
letentries;
try{
entries=awaitfs.readdir(current,{withFileTypes:true});
}catch{
return;
}
for(constentofentries){
if(isHidden(ent.name))continue;
constfull=path.join(current,ent.name);
if(ent.isDirectory()){
folderCount+=1;
awaitwalk(full,depth+1);
}elseif(ent.isFile()){
fileCount+=1;
try{
constst=awaitfs.stat(full);
if(st.mtimeMs>latestModified)latestModified=st.mtimeMs;
}catch{
/*skipunreadable*/
}
}
}
}

awaitwalk(dir,0);
return{
fileCount,
folderCount,
latestModified:latestModified||null,
};
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
Produce the full, sorted category catalogue with per-category recursive statistics — the payload of /api/categories and the seed of the entire home page.

🧒 ELI5
"Give me the list of shelves, each with how much is on it and when it
was last restocked, nicely alphabetised." The keeper checks the room, walks every shelf in parallel, and staples the lists together in ABC order.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


await getRoot(projectRoot) — gate 1 (existence + type).

await assertCategoryStructure(root) — gate 2 (non-empty, has directories), whose Dirent[] is reused.

Map each directory ent concurrently (Promise.all over dirs.map(...)): fs.stat(full) for the folder's own mtime (try/catch → mtime = null on failure), then await summarizeTree(full) for the deep counts.

Build each category object: name and path (both the raw folder name — at depth 0 the relative path is the name), type: "directory", the summary counts, mtime as ISO, and latestModified = the tree's newest file mtime as ISO, falling back to the folder's own mtime when the tree had no stat-able files.

categories.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)) — locale-aware, stable for the UI; the frontend renders in this order.

Return the array (empty impossible: assertCategoryStructure guarantees ≥ 1).



📥 Inputs


projectRoot from server.js.

Disk: FILES_ROOT + every category subtree.



📤 Outputs


Promise of Array<{ name, path, type: "directory", fileCount, folderCount, mtime, latestModified }>, sorted by name.



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: getRoot, assertCategoryStructure, summarizeTree, fs/promises, path.

Consumed by: server.js route GET /api/categories, fetched by resource-service.js.getResources() (the only frontend caller), cached 5 minutes in sessionStorage, rendered by home.js.




server.js




assets/js/services/resource-service.js




assets/js/home.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
Every field of every object here is consumed by mapCategory in resource-service.js — cat.path becomes both the card's id and its relPath (used to build /browse/<path>/ links), and cat.fileCount / cat.folderCount / cat.latestModified become the card's stats. Rename latestModified and the home page silently shows "unknown" freshness with no error. The sort is also a UI contract: localeCompare is locale-dependent, so changing to a plain < comparison visibly reorders cards for non-ASCII category names. Finally, cost: this endpoint's latency is the sum of the largest subtree; caching decisions belong in the frontend layer (which already has one), not here.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctionlistCategories(projectRoot){
constroot=awaitgetRoot(projectRoot);
constdirs=awaitassertCategoryStructure(root);

constcategories=awaitPromise.all(
dirs.map(async(ent)=>{
constfull=path.join(root,ent.name);
letmtime=null;
try{
constst=awaitfs.stat(full);
mtime=st.mtime.toISOString();
}catch{
mtime=null;
}
constsummary=awaitsummarizeTree(full);
return{
name:ent.name,
path:ent.name,
type:"directory",
fileCount:summary.fileCount,
folderCount:summary.folderCount,
mtime,
latestModified:summary.latestModified
?newDate(summary.latestModified).toISOString()
:mtime,
};
})
);

categories.sort((a,b)=>a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
returncategories;
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
List one directory anywhere in the tree — the payload of /api/files and /api/files/<rel>, powering both the category list and every drill-down in the browser page.

🧒 ELI5
"Show me what's in *this* room." At the root it answers with the shelf list; inside a shelf it answers with that shelf's boxes and items, each labelled with its location relative to the basement door so the visitor can ask for it later by that exact label.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


await getRoot(projectRoot) — gate 1.

resolveInsideRoot(root, relPath) — gate 3 (path safety).

Root case (parts.length === 0): reuse assertCategoryStructure(root) so the root listing obeys the same category rules (and the same 503s) as /api/categories, then map each directory with its own fs.stat (individual failures filtered out with return null), producing size-less directory rows with path = folder name. Note: this root case carries no recursive counts — the heavier stats live only in listCategories.

Non-root case: fs.stat(target); rejection → 404 NOT_FOUND (any stat error, including permission problems, reads as "not
   found"); !stat.isDirectory() → 400 NOT_A_DIRECTORY (asking to list a file is a client bug, not a missing resource).

readVisibleEntries(target) for typed, hidden-filtered entries.

Map each entry concurrently: fs.stat(full) (failure → null, filtered at the end — an entry deleted mid-walk silently vanishes rather than 500-ing the list); directories become type: "directory", size: 0; regular files become type: "file" with st.size and ISO mtime. toPosixRel(root, full) builds each path — the canonical value the client sends back later.

listed.filter(Boolean) drops skipped entries; anything neither file nor directory (sockets, FIFOs, etc.) is excluded by returning null.



📥 Inputs


projectRoot, relPath — the latter arrives either as the ?path= query (/api/files?path=…) or the URL suffix after /api/files/ (built by relFromPrefix in server.js).



📤 Outputs


Promise of Array<{ name, path, type: "directory"|"file", size, mtime }> — sorted implicitly by readdir order (alphabetical on all mainstream platforms).



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: getRoot, resolveInsideRoot, assertCategoryStructure, readVisibleEntries, toPosixRel, fs/promises, path.

Consumed by: server.js route GET /api/files[/...], fetched by resource-service.js.listFiles(relPath) while the user drills down in browse.js; each returned path round-trips into further /api/files or /api/download calls.




server.js




assets/js/services/resource-service.js




assets/js/browse.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
The path fields are the currency of the browse feature: browse.js builds ResourceService.browseHref(entry.path) links and the download button uses downloadHref(entry.path); any change to how path is built breaks every drill-down and download at once. The dual-route handling (?path= vs URL suffix) is load-bearing in server.js — if this function started rejecting URL-encoded-but-unsplit paths, deep links from browse.js break. The root-case/listCategories split matters too: this endpoint intentionally skips the expensive summarizeTree; making it "consistent" would slow every drill-down by a full-tree walk per category.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctionlistPath(projectRoot,relPath){
constroot=awaitgetRoot(projectRoot);
const{target,parts}=resolveInsideRoot(root,relPath);

if(parts.length===0){
constdirs=awaitassertCategoryStructure(root);
constlisted=awaitPromise.all(
dirs.map(async(ent)=>{
constfull=path.join(root,ent.name);
letst;
try{
st=awaitfs.stat(full);
}catch{
returnnull;
}
return{
name:ent.name,
path:ent.name,
type:"directory",
size:0,
mtime:st.mtime.toISOString(),
};
})
);
returnlisted.filter(Boolean);
}

letstat;
try{
stat=awaitfs.stat(target);
}catch{
throwserviceError(404,"NOT_FOUND","Pathnotfound.");
}
if(!stat.isDirectory()){
throwserviceError(400,"NOT_A_DIRECTORY","Pathisnotadirectory.");
}

constentries=awaitreadVisibleEntries(target);
constlisted=awaitPromise.all(
entries.map(async(ent)=>{
constfull=path.join(target,ent.name);
letst;
try{
st=awaitfs.stat(full);
}catch{
returnnull;
}
constchildRel=toPosixRel(root,full);
if(ent.isDirectory()){
return{
name:ent.name,
path:childRel,
type:"directory",
size:0,
mtime:st.mtime.toISOString(),
};
}
if(ent.isFile()){
return{
name:ent.name,
path:childRel,
type:"file",
size:st.size,
mtime:st.mtime.toISOString(),
};
}
returnnull;
})
);
returnlisted.filter(Boolean);
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
Resolve a client-supplied relative path into the exact file bundle that server.js may stream — the authorisation + metadata step of every /api/download request.

🧒 ELI5
The slip gets one last check before the forklift moves: it must name a real box (not a shelf), the box must actually be there, and the slip must not be blank. Only then does the keeper hand the desk a tag with the box's exact location, name, weight and last-touched date — the desk does the carrying.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


await getRoot(projectRoot) — gate 1.

resolveInsideRoot(root, relPath) — gate 3: this is where traversal attempts (/api/download/../.env) die with 403 PATH_TRAVERSAL.

parts.length === 0 → 400 INVALID_PATH with message "A file path
   is required." — a bare /api/download is a client bug.

fs.stat(target); rejection → 404 NOT_FOUND "File not found."

!stat.isFile() → 400 NOT_A_FILE — directories are not downloadable products; the UI never offers them, so a directory request indicates a crafted or stale link.

Return { absPath: target, fileName: parts[parts.length - 1], size: stat.size, mtime: stat.mtime }. Note the fileName comes from the sanitised segment list, not from path.basename(target) — same value, but provably free of separators; server.js puts this string straight into the Content-Disposition header via contentDisposition(), which additionally encodes non-ASCII names.



📥 Inputs


projectRoot, relPath (from ?path= or the URL suffix, decoded by server.js).



📤 Outputs


Promise of { absPath, fileName, size, mtime } (size in bytes, mtime as a Date — not ISO; the only raw Date this module exports, because streamDownload uses it for header building, not display).



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: getRoot, resolveInsideRoot, fs/promises.

Consumed by: server.js route GET/HEAD /api/download[/...], whose streamDownload(req, res, fileInfo) performs the actual fs.createReadStream and header work.

Sideways: the frontend builds these URLs via resource-service.js.downloadHref(relPath) — used by browse.js (file rows), home.js (release rows) and autocomplete.js (suggestion rows).




server.js




assets/js/services/resource-service.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
This is the gate in front of every byte the server ships. Removing the isFile check or the empty-path check turns 400s into unexpected behaviours (directory "downloads", server errors); weakening resolveInsideRoot here is an arbitrary-file-read vulnerability. The return shape is consumed field-by-field by streamDownload (Content-Length = size, Content-Disposition = fileName, createReadStream = absPath) — changing mtime to an ISO string would still work but silently changes nothing visible except future refactor confusion; renaming absPath, though, breaks the streamer immediately with undefined paths and EACCES-style stream errors.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctiongetDownloadTarget(projectRoot,relPath){
constroot=awaitgetRoot(projectRoot);
const{target,parts}=resolveInsideRoot(root,relPath);
if(parts.length===0){
throwserviceError(400,"INVALID_PATH","Afilepathisrequired.");
}
letstat;
try{
stat=awaitfs.stat(target);
}catch{
throwserviceError(404,"NOT_FOUND","Filenotfound.");
}
if(!stat.isFile()){
throwserviceError(400,"NOT_A_FILE","Requestedpathisnotafile.");
}
return{
absPath:target,
fileName:parts[parts.length-1],
size:stat.size,
mtime:stat.mtime,
};
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
Search the whole tree for entries whose name contains the query — the engine behind /api/search, the search box and the autocomplete dropdown.

🧒 ELI5
A treasure hunt with a walkie-talkie and a hard stop: walk the aisles (deepest-first stack), check every label against the clue, put matches on the trolley — but when the trolley is full (200) or you've touched 8000 boxes, radio "done, might be more!" (truncated: true) and come back.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


Gates: getRoot then assertCategoryStructure — an unconfigured or empty root yields the standard 503s instead of an empty search page.

Normalise the query once: String(query || "").trim().toLowerCase() — trimming means " firefox " finds firefox.zip; the predicate then stays cheap.

Clamp the limit: Math.min(Number(options.limit) || SEARCH_LIMIT, SEARCH_LIMIT) — server.js passes the raw ?limit= string (e.g. autocomplete.js sends 6); non-numeric falls back to 200, and no client can raise the ceiling.

Seed an explicit stack with { dir: root, rel: "", depth: 0 }. The while loop stack.pop() → depth-first, iterative — no recursion, so MAX_WALK_DEPTH is a filter (continue), not a stack-overflow risk, and deep trees cost time, not crashes.

Per popped directory: depth guard, then readdir(...,
   withFileTypes: true) in try/catch (unreadable dir → continue, skipping only that branch). scanned counts directories actually read (returned for diagnostics).

Per entry, in order: bail out of the for loop when results.length >= limit || visited >= MAX_WALK_ENTRIES (the mid-directory bail keeps the caps hard); skip hidden; visited counts every visible entry examined (the true work unit).

Directories: always pushed for descent, and additionally matched by name — so a category named "ubuntu" is itself a result, enabling "search finds the folder then browse into it".

Files: matched via nameMatches and pushed as results. Results carry { name, path, type } — note no size/mtime: search stays cheap (no per-result stat) at the cost of less info in the dropdown.

Loop terminates when the stack empties, results hits limit, or visited hits MAX_WALK_ENTRIES.

Return { results, scanned, truncated } where truncated is visited >= MAX_WALK_ENTRIES || results.length >= limit — the flag home.js-style UIs (and future UIs) can use to show "more
    results exist".



📥 Inputs


projectRoot, query (raw ?q= value), options.limit (raw ?limit= string or number).



📤 Outputs


Promise of { results: Array<{name, path, type}>, scanned: number, truncated: boolean }.



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: getRoot, assertCategoryStructure, nameMatches, isHidden, fs/promises, path, the limit constants.

Consumed by: server.js route GET /api/search, called by resource-service.js.search(query, { limit }), consumed by autocomplete.js (debounced, limit: 6 suggestions) and home.js (search page).




server.js




assets/js/services/resource-service.js




assets/js/autocomplete.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
Three contract surfaces here. (1) The response shape { results, scanned, truncated } is read by the frontend verbatim — flattening to a bare array breaks autocomplete.js on deploy. (2) DFS ordering is implicit UX: switching to BFS (stack.shift()) changes which 200 files a broad query returns and in what order — results users have learned. (3) The caps are latency insurance: raising MAX_WALK_ENTRIES on a huge tree makes the search event loop busy for seconds (each readdir is awaited but the walk is sequential), which stalls every route on the single-threaded server, not just search. Also note q is currently unused for pruning directory descent — every directory is explored regardless of name match; adding pruning changes which files inside non-matching folders are discoverable.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctionsearchEntries(projectRoot,query,options={}){
constroot=awaitgetRoot(projectRoot);
awaitassertCategoryStructure(root);
constq=String(query||"").trim().toLowerCase();
constlimit=Math.min(Number(options.limit)||SEARCH_LIMIT,SEARCH_LIMIT);
constresults=[];
letscanned=0;
letvisited=0;

conststack=[{dir:root,rel:"",depth:0}];
while(stack.length&&results.length<limit&&visited<MAX_WALK_ENTRIES){
const{dir,rel,depth}=stack.pop();
if(depth>MAX_WALK_DEPTH)continue;
letentries;
try{
entries=awaitfs.readdir(dir,{withFileTypes:true});
}catch{
continue;
}
scanned+=1;
for(constentofentries){
if(results.length>=limit||visited>=MAX_WALK_ENTRIES)break;
if(isHidden(ent.name))continue;
visited+=1;
constchildRel=rel?`${rel}/${ent.name}`:ent.name;
constfull=path.join(dir,ent.name);
if(ent.isDirectory()){
stack.push({dir:full,rel:childRel,depth:depth+1});
if(nameMatches(ent.name,q)){
results.push({
name:ent.name,
path:childRel,
type:"directory",
});
}
}elseif(ent.isFile()&&nameMatches(ent.name,q)){
results.push({
name:ent.name,
path:childRel,
type:"file",
});
}
}
}

return{results,scanned,truncated:visited>=MAX_WALK_ENTRIES||results.length>=limit};
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
Walk the entire tree and return the most recently modified files across all categories — the "Latest releases" feed of /api/releases shown on the home page.

🧒 ELI5
"Show me the twelve newest things anywhere in the building." The keeper walks every shelf (no shortcut), notes each item's date, sorts the whole pile newest-first, and hands over just the top of the pile.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


Gates: getRoot + assertCategoryStructure (same 503 contract as search).

Clamp the cap: Math.min(Number(limit) || RELEASES_LIMIT, 50) — default 12 (RELEASES_LIMIT), hard maximum 50. server.js passes the raw ?limit= string; resource-service.js's getReleases default is 8.

Recursive async walk(dir, rel, depth): guards on depth > MAX_WALK_DEPTH or visited >= MAX_WALK_ENTRIES (return early — the entry budget is global, not per-branch); readdir in try/catch (unreadable branch → skip); hidden entries skipped; directories recurse with the extended POSIX rel prefix.

Files: fs.stat in try/catch (vanishing file → skip); push { name, path, category, type: "file", size, mtime }. Note the extra category field — childRel.split("/")[0] — which no other function provides; it lets the home page badge each release with its category name without a second lookup.

After the walk: files.sort((a, b) => new Date(b.mtime) - new Date(a.mtime)) — newest first (note b - a: descending). Sorting everything before slicing is what makes this a correct global "latest" rather than "latest per folder".

return files.slice(0, cap).



📥 Inputs


projectRoot, limit (raw ?limit= value or default).



📤 Outputs


Promise of Array<{ name, path, category, type: "file", size, mtime }> (ISO mtime strings), newest first, length ≤ min(requested, 50).



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: getRoot, assertCategoryStructure, isHidden, fs/promises, path, RELEASES_LIMIT, MAX_WALK_DEPTH, MAX_WALK_ENTRIES.

Consumed by: server.js route GET /api/releases, fetched by resource-service.js.getReleases(limit) and rendered by home.js as the "latest releases" section (each row's path feeds downloadHref).




server.js




assets/js/services/resource-service.js




assets/js/home.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
The category field is exclusive to this endpoint — dropping it breaks the home page's category labels, not just types. Sorting happens on the full visited set before the cap: a tree with hundreds of thousands of files holds them all in memory briefly per request (this endpoint is the most expensive in the module — no early exit possible, since "latest" is only known after seeing everything). The 50-hard-cap means a client asking limit=1000 silently gets 50 — intentionally, but any frontend change relying on larger feeds must raise the constant here. truncated is not reported by this function (unlike searchEntries); on trees that exceed MAX_WALK_ENTRIES the feed silently covers only the first-visited portion — a subtle correctness boundary worth knowing before touching the walk.

💻 Live Source Code:
asyncfunctionlistLatestReleases(projectRoot,limit=RELEASES_LIMIT){
constroot=awaitgetRoot(projectRoot);
awaitassertCategoryStructure(root);
constcap=Math.min(Number(limit)||RELEASES_LIMIT,50);
constfiles=[];
letvisited=0;

asyncfunctionwalk(dir,rel,depth){
if(depth>MAX_WALK_DEPTH||visited>=MAX_WALK_ENTRIES)return;
letentries;
try{
entries=awaitfs.readdir(dir,{withFileTypes:true});
}catch{
return;
}
for(constentofentries){
if(visited>=MAX_WALK_ENTRIES)return;
if(isHidden(ent.name))continue;
visited+=1;
constchildRel=rel?`${rel}/${ent.name}`:ent.name;
constfull=path.join(dir,ent.name);
if(ent.isDirectory()){
awaitwalk(full,childRel,depth+1);
}elseif(ent.isFile()){
try{
constst=awaitfs.stat(full);
files.push({
name:ent.name,
path:childRel,
category:childRel.split("/")[0]||"",
type:"file",
size:st.size,
mtime:st.mtime.toISOString(),
});
}catch{
/*skip*/
}
}
}
}

awaitwalk(root,"",0);
files.sort((a,b)=>newDate(b.mtime)-newDate(a.mtime));
returnfiles.slice(0,cap);
}


─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

🎯 Purpose
A synchronous, never-throwing snapshot of FILES_ROOT health for two callers that cannot await: server.js's startup banner and the /api/health (and /__health) endpoint.

🧒 ELI5
A five-second hallway check with a guarantee: "I will come back with an
answer, even if the answer is 'I couldn't even check'." It never shouts, never refuses to answer — it just reports three yes/no facts and, if it failed, why as a code.

⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)


Wrap everything in try/catch — this is the module's only never-throw public function (health probes must never 500).

Inside try: resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot) (throws FILES_ROOT_MISSING if unset), then fsSync.existsSync(root) — the sync API is deliberate: the startup banner runs inside server.listen's callback, and the health handler wants a one-shot answer without async plumbing in its hot path.

isDir via fsSync.statSync(root).isDirectory(), guarded by exists && so a missing root never triggers a second throw.

Return the happy shape: { configured: true, exists, isDirectory }.

In catch (err): return the diagnostic shape — configured: err.code !== "FILES_ROOT_MISSING" (distinguishing "not
   configured" from "configured but broken"), exists: false, isDirectory: false, and error: err.code || "UNKNOWN" — the serviceError code, which is precisely why the codes are stable identifiers.



📥 Inputs


projectRoot; environment FILES_ROOT; disk stat of the root (sync).



📤 Outputs


{ configured, exists, isDirectory } or { configured, exists: false, isDirectory: false, error } — plain data, never throws.



🔗 Project Dependencies


Depends on: resolveConfiguredRoot, fsSync (callback fs module), and indirectly on serviceError's codes.

Consumed by: server.js twice — inside the /api/health handler (the status: "ok" | "degraded", filesRootConfigured, filesRootReady fields are computed directly from this result) and in the server.listen startup banner (the green/yellow FILES_ROOT is… line).




server.js

⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
The result shape is read field-by-field by server.js's health JSON — uptime monitors and dashboards key on status: "ok" which is computed as configured && exists && isDirectory from these three booleans. Changing field names flips production health checks to "degraded" with zero code errors. Removing the catch would violate the never-throw contract: /api/health would fall into sendServiceError and start 500-ing exactly when operators need it most. And using the promise fs here would change the return type to a Promise, silently making every boolean field undefined (truthiness checks in the banner would still "work", producing confidently wrong health reports).

💻 Live Source Code:
functionpeekRootSync(projectRoot){
try{
constroot=resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot);
constexists=fsSync.existsSync(root);
constisDir=exists&&fsSync.statSync(root).isDirectory();
return{configured:true,exists,isDirectory:isDir};
}catch(err){
return{
configured:err.code!=="FILES_ROOT_MISSING",
exists:false,
isDirectory:false,
error:err.code||"UNKNOWN",
};
}
}


